On Monday 11 April 2011 13:32:49 Ian Johnson wrote:
> One problem with {va'o da'i} is that it is ambiguous with respect to
> counterfactuality, in that events which are hypothetical are permitted to
> happen. In English, we have "If ... then ..." vs. "Were it true that ...
> then ...". (My example is clunky because it's generic; in practice it's n=
ot
> necessarily that clunky.) In some Romance languages we have different
> subjunctive tenses for this distinction: Latin would use "si <present
> subjunctive> <indicative>" for the non-counterfactual case and "si
> <imperfect subjunctive> <indicative>" for the counterfactual case. How ca=
n
> Lojban make this distinction?

"da'i ru'e" vs. "da'i sai". Btw, "were" in "were it" is the imperfect=20
subjunctive, a form that in all other English verbs is indistinguishable fr=
om=20
the past.

Greek indicates the distinction another way. "=C5=E1=ED =F4=E1=E9=F2 =E3=EB=
=F9=F3=F3=E1=E9=F2 =F4=F9=ED =E1=ED=E8=F1=F9=F0=F9=ED=20
=EB=E1=EB=F9 =EA=E1=E9 =F4=F9=ED =E1=E3=E3=E5=EB=F9=ED, =E1=E3=E1=F0=E7=ED =
=E4=E5 =EC=E7 =E5=F7=F9, =E3=E5=E3=EF=ED=E1 =F7=E1=EB=EA=EF=F2 =E7=F7=F9=ED=
 =E7 =EA=F5=EC=E2=E1=EB=EF=ED=20
=E1=EB=E1=EB=E1=E6=EF=ED." (1 Cor. 13:1) The verb is in present subjunctive=
 (can't tell from=20
=EB=E1=EB=F9 that it's subjunctive, but some succeeding verbs make it clear=
); the=20
impossibility of the counterfactual is indicated by using "=E5=E1=ED" ("ife=
ver")=20
instead of "=E5=E9" ("if"). The Reina-Valera puts the verbs all in past=20
subjunctive; I think the one in verse 3 should be in future subjunctive (th=
e=20
Greek =F8=F9=EC=E9=F3=F9 is aorist subjunctive, which looks like future). T=
he NVI has=20
present indicative, and the NIV has present, but I can't tell whether they'=
re=20
indicative or subjunctive. Here's an attempt in Lojban:

lo nu tavla sebau da'isai lo remna .e lo .angeli gi'enai prami cu se jalge =
lo=20
nu binxo lo snaminra lastu .a lo cmoni kimvalo

(Which reminds me: I need to create the git repo. I have the conversion scr=
ipt=20
working.)

An interesting use of =E5=E9 is in Hebrews 3:11: =D9=F2 =F9=EC=EF=F3=E1 =E5=
=ED =F4=E7 =EF=F1=E3=E7 =EC=EF=F5, "=C5=E9=20
=E5=E9=F3=E5=EB=E5=F5=F3=EF=ED=F4=E1=E9 =E5=E9=F2 =E5=E7=ED =EA=E1=F4=E1=F0=
=E1=F5=F3=E9=ED =EC=EF=F5". (As I swore in my wrath, "If they will=20
enter my rest".) The closest English construction (a strong prohibition cas=
t=20
as a logical connection with one part missing) is "You stay out of my rest,=
=20
or else!"

